\pard\sb0\sa100\b\fs26 Privacy policy\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 Effective {\b 2026-09-26}. It covers three things that are easy to confuse: the {\b mindfork application}, the website {\b mindfork.io}, and the project's presence on {\b GitHub}. They have very different answers, so they are kept apart below.\par
\pard\sa120 {\b The short version.} mindfork is a local program. It has no telemetry, no analytics, no crash reporting, no update check and no account. Nothing it does reports back to the author, and there is no server of this project's for it to report to. Everything it stores stays in its data folder on your computer (\u167?2), apart from the short-lived working files listed in \u167?8. It talks to the network for the model you configured, for the tools that are switched on and for the setup commands you run \u8212? and where it does, your data goes to {\i that} provider, under {\i their} policy, never through anything of ours.\par
\pard\sa120 The rest of this document is the detailed version, written from the code rather than from a template, including the parts that are on by default. Its companions: SECURITY.md (what the app promises, and how to report a break), DISCLAIMER.md \u167?5 (what it means, legally, that a request leaves your machine), and docs/install.md (where the data directory is on each platform).\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 1. Who is responsible for what\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 The software is published by {\b Vladimir Shylov}, its author and copyright holder, as free open-source software under the MIT License.\par
\pard\sa120 For the data the application handles on your computer, {\b you} are the only party with access to it. The author operates no service that receives it and cannot obtain it. When you point the app at a provider, that provider becomes responsible for what you send them, on the terms you accepted with them; the author is not a party to that relationship and has no visibility into it.\par
\pard\sa120 For the website and the GitHub repository the author does make the choices \u8212? see \u167?9 and \u167?10, where both are deliberately kept as thin as they can be.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 2. What stays on your machine\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 The app is {\b portable by design}: its data lives in a {\f1 data/} directory next to the executable, not in a hidden per-user location, unless a {\f1 defaults.json} next to the binary selects a system location or another path (docs/install.md). In a development build that is {\f1 target/debug/data/}. So everything the app knows is in one place you can open, copy, back up and delete. On Linux that directory is made readable {\b to your user alone} ({\f1 0700}) every time the app starts, and the files it writes are {\f1 0600}: before, the system's default left both open to anyone else with an account on the machine. On Windows the profile's own permissions do that.\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b Settings, and the encrypted secrets map \u8212? each stored key labelled with the name of the computer it was entered on} \u8212? {\i Where:} {\f1 settings.json} \u8212? {\i Holds your conversations:} no\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b Profiles, system prompts, per-profile tool lists} \u8212? {\i Where:} {\f1 profiles.json} \u8212? {\i Holds your conversations:} your prompts\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b Chats} \u8212? {\i Where:} {\f1 chats/<uuid>.json} \u8212? messages, model thoughts, tool calls and their results, sub-agent transcripts, attached images inline, attachment text \u8212? {\i Holds your conversations:} {\b yes}\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b Notes, the RAG knowledge base and their embedding vectors, the self-model, the record of which model ran when} \u8212? {\i Where:} {\f1 data.db} (SQLite) \u8212? {\i Holds your conversations:} {\b yes}\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b The full-text search index} \u8212? {\i Where:} {\f1 cache.db} (SQLite) \u8212? {\i Holds your conversations:} {\b yes} \u8212? derived; delete it and it rebuilds\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b Pre-images of every project file the assistant edited, for the {\f1 F4} diff and revert} \u8212? {\i Where:} {\f1 workspace/<chat-id>/} \u8212? {\i Holds your conversations:} your source code\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b Files kept with a chat \u8212? what the Python sandbox saved (charts, tables, workbooks), and files you attached with {\f1 /file attach} whose original is kept: binaries, and the PDF, DOCX or HTML beside its extracted text} \u8212? {\i Where:} {\f1 files/<chat-id>/} \u8212? {\i Holds your conversations:} {\b yes} \u8212? your files, and what the code made of them\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b Backups} \u8212? {\i Where:} {\f1 backups/}, or the location you choose \u8212? {\i Holds your conversations:} yes \u8212? see \u167?7\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b Diagnostics} \u8212? {\i Where:} {\f1 logs/} \u8212? {\i Holds your conversations:} no \u8212? see \u167?6\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b The Python sandbox runtime} \u8212? {\i Where:} {\f1 sandbox/} \u8212? {\i Holds your conversations:} no\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b llama.cpp builds downloaded by {\f1 mindfork llama setup}} \u8212? {\i Where:} {\f1 llama/} \u8212? {\i Holds your conversations:} no\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b Spellcheck dictionaries, locales, the words you added yourself} \u8212? {\i Where:} {\f1 dictionaries/}, {\f1 locales/}, {\f1 personal_dictionary.txt} \u8212? {\i Holds your conversations:} the personal dictionary holds words you added\par
\pard\sa120 None of this is uploaded anywhere by the app. Copying the folder to another machine carries your chats with it \u8212? and deliberately does {\i not} carry usable secrets (\u167?7).\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 3. What leaves your machine\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 Every outbound connection is to an endpoint {\b you} configured, to a tool endpoint that is part of a feature that is switched on, or to the download host of a setup command you ran. There is no destination the app contacts on its own initiative, and no request is ever made about you, your machine or your usage. What follows is the complete list.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 3.1 The model\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 Where your conversation goes depends on the engine mode you chose:\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b Managed} \u8212? the app starts {\f1 llama-server} on your own machine and talks to {\f1 127.0.0.1} only. Nothing leaves the computer, whatever the server was told to bind to.\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b External} \u8212? the OpenAI-compatible URL you entered, and nowhere else.\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b Cloud} \u8212? the provider you selected: {\f1 api.openai.com}, {\f1 generativelanguage.googleapis.com} (Google Gemini), {\f1 api.anthropic.com}, {\f1 api.x.ai} (Grok), or a different base URL if you set one. Each processes your data under its own policy \u8212? OpenAI, Google, Anthropic, xAI \u8212? and their retention and training-on-your-data rules are theirs, not ours. Read them.\par
\pard\sa120 {\b Impersonation} \u8212? the model drafting your next message \u8212? has an engine setting of its own. By default it shares the chat engine; pointed elsewhere, it sends the conversation to that external server or cloud provider instead.\par
\pard\sa120 The {\f1 MINDFORK_ENGINE_URL}, {\f1 MINDFORK_LLAMA_BIN}, {\f1 MINDFORK_EMBED_URL} and {\f1 MINDFORK_EMBED_BIN} environment variables, when set, replace the chat or embedding engine in the settings for that launch (docs/install.md).\par
\pard\sa120 {\b What a request contains:} the conversation so far (a chat model is stateless, so the history is re-sent every turn), the system prompt \u8212? including the self-model text if those tools are enabled, and the text of chat attachments, which are re-injected each turn \u8212? images encoded inline, the schemas of the tools that are on, and the results of tool calls. Anything a tool retrieved therefore becomes part of the history: a note recalled by {\f1 note_recall} or a passage found by {\f1 rag_search} travels with every subsequent turn of that conversation.\par
\pard\sa120 {\b Requests you did not type.} Some features ask the model on your behalf, and they are worth knowing about because they are ordinary requests to the same provider: naming a new chat (on by default, after the first substantive reply), compacting a long history into a rolling summary, summarizing a page that {\f1 fetch_url} retrieved, sub-agents and {\f1 run_dialogue}, and \u8212? only when you turn them on \u8212? reflection and the notes/self-model consolidation passes.\par
\pard\sa120 {\b Probes.} In managed and external mode the app asks the server {\f1 /health} and {\f1 /props}, and {\f1 GET /models} for what it is running \u8212? the model's name, context window, image support and accepted parameters. The Grok cloud is asked the same {\f1 /props} and {\f1 /models}, with your key, when the engine is set up and when you attach an image. The OpenAI, Gemini and Anthropic clouds are not probed.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 3.2 Embeddings (notes, RAG, search reranking)\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 The embedding endpoint is a {\b separate setting} from the chat one: it can be a local server, an external URL, or a cloud provider \u8212? which may well be a {\i different} vendor from the one running your chat.\par
\pard\sa120 What gets embedded, and therefore sent there: note text, self-model traits, chunks of documents you added to the knowledge base (including text extracted from PDF and DOCX), chunks of chat attachments, the queries of {\f1 rag_search}, {\f1 note_recall} and {\f1 attachment_search} \u8212? and, less obviously, {\b {\f1 web_search} queries together with up to 800 characters of each result page}, which the tool embeds to rank results. If your embedder is a cloud provider, that is search content reaching a second vendor.\par
\pard\sa120 To notice that the embedding model has changed, the app also embeds one fixed sentence ({\f1 mindfork embedding canary v1}) when it first uses the embedder in a session, and a fixed set of calibration sentences once for each new model. Neither contains anything of yours.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 3.3 The web tools \u8212? off until you turn them on\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 {\f1 tools.web_enabled} is {\b off in a fresh installation}, and with it {\f1 web_search}, {\f1 fetch_url} and {\f1 youtube_watch}. Everything in this section happens only after you switch it on in Settings \u8594? Tools. (It shipped {\i on} before 0.9.9; an installation that already has a {\f1 settings.json} keeps whatever that file says, so if you had it on, it stays on.)\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b {\f1 web_search}} queries, in order, {\f1 lite.duckduckgo.com}, {\f1 html.duckduckgo.com}, {\f1 www.mojeek.com} and {\f1 www.ecosia.org}. What is sent is the search string the model composed \u8212? which is derived from your conversation \u8212? plus a desktop browser User-Agent and an {\f1 Accept-Language} header. These engines are chosen by the app, not by you.\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b A keyed provider} is preferred over that chain when a key is available: today that is Tavily ({\f1 api.tavily.com}). A key is only ever found where you put it \u8212? entered in settings, or in an environment variable {\b you named} in settings. No variable name is assumed, here or anywhere else in the app: before 0.9.9 the app looked for {\f1 TAVILY_API_KEY} by default, which meant a key exported for some unrelated tool could route your searches, and spend its credits, without a decision made anywhere in this app.\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b Result pages are fetched} by default ({\f1 tools.web_fetch_content}) to extract readable text, so the search engines' hits are visited too.\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b {\f1 fetch_url}} retrieves the address the model chose. A page that fits the attachment budget reaches your chat model as a summary \u8212? up to 12 000 characters of it are sent to be summarized \u8212? or as its text, when the model asks for no summary or summarizing fails. A larger page (up to 1 000 000 characters) is attached to the chat and kept with it, and its passages travel like any attachment's (\u167?3.1).\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b {\f1 youtube_watch}} fetches the video's watch page \u8212? or, failing that, its oEmbed record \u8212? from {\f1 www.youtube.com} for the title and length, and, if a Gemini key is available \u8212? the one saved for the Gemini provider, or the variable you named in the video settings \u8212? hands the URL to Gemini, which watches the video on its own servers. No video bytes leave your machine; the URL and the prompt do.\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b Where they may not go}: model-chosen addresses are resolved and checked against the routable public internet \u8212? your LAN, loopback and other non-routable addresses are refused, on the original request and on every redirect, unless you set {\f1 tools.web_allow_private}.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 3.4 Speech\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 Nothing is ever spoken automatically: text is sent only when you invoke {\f1 /tts}. When you do, the {\b default provider is OpenAI's cloud} ({\f1 api.openai.com}), with Gemini and any OpenAI-compatible server as alternatives. What is sent is the message text, flattened out of markdown and split into chunks.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 3.5 Images you attach by URL\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 {\f1 /image attach <url>} downloads the image with its own client and re-encodes it locally; the address is never handed to a model provider to fetch. Only {\f1 http} and {\f1 https} addresses are accepted. Because you typed the address yourself, it is not subject to the public-address guard in \u167?3.3 \u8212? an intranet or loopback address is your decision, not a model's.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 3.6 MCP servers\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 MCP servers run as {\b local subprocesses} \u8212? the app speaks to them over the process's own pipes, not over the network. What leaves the app is whatever the model puts in a tool call's arguments; what the server then does with it, and where it sends it, is that third party's business and is covered by their terms, not this policy. Tokens you store for a server are passed to it as environment variables \u8212? and the server process also inherits the rest of the environment mindfork was started with, including any API keys exported in that shell. MCP is a double opt-in: a master switch plus per-profile approval, both off by default, and a server that changes its tool set after you approved it has to be approved again.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 3.7 The Python sandbox's one-time setup\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 {\f1 mindfork sandbox setup} is an explicit command, run once. It downloads the {\f1 wasmer} runtime from its GitHub release page and 34 Python wheels from {\f1 files.pythonhosted.org} and {\f1 pythonindex.wasix.org}; each of those is {\b verified against a sha256 lock list} before use. It then runs the downloaded {\f1 wasmer} binary to fetch the Python distribution ({\f1 python.webc}, the largest piece) from the Wasmer registry. That one is fetched by {\f1 wasmer} rather than by this app, so there is no URL for the lock list to pin \u8212? instead {\b the finished file is checked against a pinned digest}, and one that does not match is replaced rather than used. What that third-party binary contacts beyond this is outside our control. Nothing about your data is sent in any of it; it is a package download.\par
\pard\sa120 If a {\f1 wasmer} runtime is present but the setup never finished, a Python run can make {\f1 wasmer} fetch the plain Python package from the Wasmer registry itself.\par
\pard\sa120 Once Python execution is enabled, the sandbox's own network access is on by default ({\f1 tools.python_net_enabled}), which means code the model runs can reach the internet from inside the sandbox \u8212? {\b public addresses only}: this machine's own services, your local network, the link-local range where cloud metadata lives and the other non-routable ranges are refused, the same list the web tools use, unless you set {\f1 tools.web_allow_private}. In the {\b local} Python mode the code runs with your own interpreter and has whatever network access your computer has; that switch does not apply to it.\par
\pard\sa120 The code the model writes also runs {\b without the credentials in the app's environment}: variables whose names look like keys ({\f1 *_KEY}, {\f1 *TOKEN*}, {\f1 *SECRET*}, {\f1 *PASSWORD*}, \u8230?) and whatever your settings name as a key source are removed before the local interpreter \u8212? or a command of the code workspace \u8212? is started. It is a narrowing, not a boundary: a secret under an unrecognisable name still travels, and any code running as you can read your credential {\i files}.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 3.8 The llama.cpp download\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 {\f1 mindfork llama backends} and {\f1 mindfork llama setup} are explicit commands. They ask {\f1 api.github.com} for the release list of the {\f1 ggml-org/llama.cpp} repository, and {\f1 setup} downloads the build you picked \u8212? and, for a CUDA build, its runtime archive \u8212? from that release on GitHub, checking each file against the sha256 GitHub publishes for it. The requests carry the User-Agent {\f1 mindfork-llama-setup} and no credentials: a {\f1 GITHUB_TOKEN} in your environment is not read.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 3.9 The clipboard over SSH\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 When copying uses OSC 52 \u8212? automatically over a remote session or when the local clipboard cannot be reached, or always if you set it \u8212? the copied text is handed to the terminal, and over a remote session it travels the SSH connection to the machine you are sitting at. That is the purpose of the feature, and it is worth knowing that the text passes through whatever is between you and the host.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 4. What is off until you turn it on\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 {\b Off until you say otherwise:}\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab the {\b web tools} \u8212? search, page fetching, YouTube (\u167?3.3);\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b Python execution} ({\f1 tools.python_enabled});\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab the {\b file tools} ({\f1 tools.fs_enabled}) \u8212? and they work only inside the folder you set as their root ({\f1 fs_root}): until you set one they refuse, and the app's own data and program folders stay out of their reach either way;\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b MCP plugins} \u8212? the master switch and the per-profile one, both;\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b cross-chat search} for the assistant ({\f1 chat_search}, {\f1 chat_read});\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab the {\b self-model tools} \u8212? and with them the injection of the self-model into the system prompt;\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b reflection} and the consolidation passes;\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab reaching {\b private or loopback addresses} from the web tools;\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab the {\b confirmation prompt} before a dangerous tool call ({\f1 tools.confirm_dangerous}) \u8212? it is opt-in, so tools you have enabled run without asking.\par
\pard\sa120 {\b On unless you turn them off:}\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab fetching the {\b content of search results}, once the web tools are on;\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab preferring a {\b keyed search provider}, once you have said where its key lives;\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b naming a new chat} automatically, which is one extra model request;\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b compacting a long history} into a rolling summary once it nears the model's context window, which is one extra model request each time;\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b showing the model the images a tool produced} \u8212? charts from Python, images from MCP tools \u8212? once those tools are on;\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\b network access inside the Python sandbox}, once Python is enabled;\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab the cloud as the {\b default speech provider} \u8212? though nothing is spoken until you invoke {\f1 /tts};\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab the ordinary local tools: notes, the knowledge base, the time, and so on.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 5. What the author receives\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 Nothing. There is no telemetry, no analytics, no usage statistics, no crash or error reporting, no update check, no version ping, no license check, no account and no server operated by this project. The addresses of the website, the repository and the crates.io page that appear in the "About" dialog are text on a screen; nothing fetches them.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 6. Logs\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 The app writes diagnostics to {\f1 logs/}, one file a day ({\f1 mindfork.log.YYYY-MM-DD}), on your machine only.\par
\pard\sa120 {\b No message text, no prompt text and no API key is written to them.} What is recorded is identifiers, counts, statuses, timings, model names, endpoint URLs, error strings, and the paths and names of files you added to the knowledge base or attached to a chat. Two things can carry third-party text into a log: the error body a provider returned when a request failed, and lines an MCP server you connected printed outside its protocol (its error output as well, if you raise the level). At the default level, the URLs the web tools fetched are not logged; they appear if you raise the level with {\f1 MINDFORK_LOG}.\par
\pard\sa120 Log files are {\b not pruned}: they accumulate until you delete them. They are excluded from backups.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 7. Secrets, and backups\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 {\b Secrets.} Keys you enter in settings \u8212? cloud provider keys, external-server keys, MCP tokens, a search API key, the backup password \u8212? are stored inside {\f1 settings.json} {\b encrypted and bound to the machine}: DPAPI on Windows, and on Linux a key derived from the host's machine id and your user name, with ChaCha20-Poly1305. A settings file copied to another computer therefore carries no usable secret. The design and, importantly, its limits are in ADR 0008: it defends against a settings file carried off to another machine, not against code running as you on your own. As an alternative you can store the {\i name} of an environment variable instead of a key, in which case the app never holds the value at all. Keys are sent only to the service they belong to.\par
\pard\sa120 {\b Backups} contain settings, profiles, your personal dictionary, a copy of {\f1 data.db}, the whole {\f1 chats/} directory \u8212? that is, your conversations \u8212? the files kept with chats ({\f1 files/}), the copies of project files the assistant edited ({\f1 workspace/}), your dictionaries and locale overrides, the {\f1 .bak} copies of all of these, and the file tools' folder when it lies inside the data folder. They exclude logs and previous backups. A backup can be encrypted with a password (AES-256); note that even then the archive's manifest, entry names and sizes remain readable without it. Where you put a backup, and who can read it, is your decision \u8212? an unencrypted backup is a copy of everything.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 8. Files the app can write outside its own folder\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 For completeness, since "everything lives in {\f1 data/}" is true of the app's own storage but not of everything it can do at your request:\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab a Python run, in either mode, gets a working folder under the system temporary directory ({\f1 mindfork-sbx-<id>}) holding the model's code and copies of the chat files the call named; it is removed after the run, and one left behind by a crash is removed 24 hours later. The interpreter is given the script's path, not the code;\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\f1 /export} writes a conversation to the path you give, or to a generated file name in the current directory;\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab the file tools and the code-workspace tools write where the model asks, inside {\f1 fs_root} or the attached project directory \u8212? never into the app's own folders or a project's {\f1 .git/};\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab {\f1 mindfork demo} provisions a throwaway data directory under the system temporary folder and removes it on exit;\par
\pard\fi-280\li280\sa80 \bullet\tab copying puts text on the operating system clipboard, which other applications can read.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 9. The website, mindfork.io\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 The site is {\b static} \u8212? pages generated ahead of time, served from Amazon S3 through CloudFront. It has {\b no analytics} of any kind, {\b no cookies} set by us and therefore no cookie banner, {\b no forms}, no sign-up, no comments, and {\b no third-party embeds}: fonts, styles and images come from the site's own origin.\par
\pard\sa120 The infrastructure that serves it (infra/website.cfn.yaml) configures {\b no access logging} \u8212? neither the bucket nor the distribution is set up to deliver or retain request logs. Amazon Web Services is the hosting provider and processes requests in order to serve them, under its own terms. While the site is restricted to an IP allowlist during maintenance, a small function compares the requesting address against that list at request time; it stores nothing.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 10. GitHub\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 The source code, the issue tracker and the release downloads are hosted by {\b GitHub}, so reading a page, opening an issue or downloading a release is subject to GitHub's privacy statement, not this one. The author sees what any repository owner sees: public activity on the repository and the contents of what you post there. Please do not put personal data into an issue \u8212? a bug report needs a version, an operating system and a reproduction, never your chat history. Security reports have their own private channel (SECURITY.md).\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 11. Donations\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 If and when the project accepts donations, the donation platform handles the payment and your payment details; the author never sees a card number. What reaches the author is what such platforms show a recipient \u8212? a name or handle, the amount, the date, and any message you attached. It is used only to acknowledge the donation and to keep the project's accounts, is not combined with anything else, is never sold or shared, and is not used to contact you about anything you did not ask for.\par
\pard\sa120 Donating is never required to use, obtain or update the software, and no feature is withheld from anyone who does not.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 12. Deleting everything\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 Because the app is portable, deletion is a file operation and not a request to anybody: remove the {\f1 data/} directory next to the executable, and any backup or export you made elsewhere, and nothing of yours remains. Removing {\f1 cache.db} alone just drops the derived search index. There is no account to close and nothing of yours on any server of this project's.\par
\pard\sa120 Data you sent to a model or search provider is held by them, and only they can delete it, under their own retention rules. The same is true of anything you posted on GitHub.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 13. Changes to this policy\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 The policy is versioned with the source: it lives in the repository, changes through pull requests, and its history is public \u8212? so you can diff it. The date at the top marks the current version. A change that widens what the software sends anywhere is a user-visible change and would also appear in CHANGELOG.md.\par
\pard\sb200\sa100\b\fs22 14. Contact\b0\fs18\par
\pard\sa120 Questions about this policy, or about what the software does with anything: open an issue on the repository, or write to the project's maintainer address, {\f1 vladimir.shylov@outlook.com} \u8212? the same address that identifies the maintainer of the Linux packages. For anything that is actually a security problem, use the private channel described in SECURITY.md rather than a public issue.\par
}
